#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228896 :question/id #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21615" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370 :topic/math-academy-id 6678 :topic/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "1d103008-5f2b-56d3-bf46-9acb13be1e14" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228039}] :question/id #uuid "7189602a-9b03-5e22-b3c9-dc1572c67ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332393" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$9\\frac{dy}{dx} + 18y = 4e^{2x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "5a1c3645-f231-5b72-8d48-7f5f47936ca9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228080}] :question/id #uuid "08f65c9c-ef2f-502a-89da-22e35becf913" :question/math-academy-id "q-332645" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$5\\frac{dy}{dx} + 10y = 15e^{-3x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3df8082-e465-5116-a85f-68df826412e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3df8082-e465-5116-a85f-68df826412e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303657} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e98a4e4-c028-589a-8b58-7ac0d0bcae34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304460}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0a6279e-623c-5e5d-bfce-194a9b63b40e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332644" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=9{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (12-6)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244173e6-1d98-5cad-87c9-7af175491718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "244173e6-1d98-5cad-87c9-7af175491718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302685} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d9f51d-96fd-58e9-9095-b4dd104ad45c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304113}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e8b85e6-cf06-5623-ab71-a62bf6c263eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-332655" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-20y=15{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15{e}^{-x},$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})-20(\\alpha {e}^{-x}) & =15{e}^{-x} \\\\ (-5-20)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =15{e}^{-x} \\\\ -25\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =15{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{-x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2aa0baf-8ec9-5192-85c0-603f29d4e762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2aa0baf-8ec9-5192-85c0-603f29d4e762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303460} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86331a1-fbe8-5a67-9321-0a3def4ead2b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304670}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ab75a0-6cd5-582d-bf4b-82f239086002" :question/math-academy-id "q-332659" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-18y=24{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $24{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})-18(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (-12-18)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -30\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e6caee-5ce8-5b54-b11e-bee12abdd01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02e6caee-5ce8-5b54-b11e-bee12abdd01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302474} :answer-field/id #uuid "d923e6d5-a40b-5eda-9b0c-429466506414" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81fa1809-9925-54c1-9732-91934088476a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332664" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+14y=21{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $21{e}^{-5x},$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x})+14(\\alpha {e}^{-5x}) & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (-35+14)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ -21\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-5x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d5105ee-fe34-54db-8a0a-21ab91d763f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5105ee-fe34-54db-8a0a-21ab91d763f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303337} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c1a6e5-7995-5568-aa72-550a639d2bbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304539}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1545356e-4e65-553c-8118-fc733c23efcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-332667" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x} \\\\ (8\\alpha +8\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(12\\beta +8\\beta ){e}^{3x} & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x} \\\\ 16\\alpha {e}^{2x}+20\\beta {e}^{3x} & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n16\\alpha =5,\\,\\,20\\beta =3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{5}{16},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{16}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}{e}^{3x}.\n$$"} {:db/id