\\\\ (3\\alpha +9\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(12\\beta +9\\beta ){e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 12\\alpha {e}^{x}+21\\beta {e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{4x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n12\\alpha =7\\,\\text{and}\\,21\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{7}{12}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{21}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{7}{12}{e}^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa57c06-4f06-5e73-85e6-7ea49cfe4b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa57c06-4f06-5e73-85e6-7ea49cfe4b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303629} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cb2b8b4-f8fc-59fd-979c-5094044e61f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304286}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6adfa911-36fb-57ce-b523-2914b62bca69" :question/math-academy-id "q-332669" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x}) & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ (2\\alpha +3\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(5\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x}+8\\beta {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{5x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n5\\alpha =2,\\,8\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e819727d-0c39-59a5-a1aa-de8b1e553484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e819727d-0c39-59a5-a1aa-de8b1e553484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303898} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d8de7e-fd86-518d-8a26-54633946daa5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304626}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "945e99ed-3594-5be0-95b2-a2681c44f43f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332670" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(4\\beta -6\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{x}-2\\beta {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{2x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha =3,\\,\\,-2\\beta =-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\,\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :question/id #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" :question/math-academy-id "e-21621" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813 :topic/math-academy-id 1109 :topic/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69474fc7-c867-5853-a468-3bbf94c86c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69474fc7-c867-5853-a468-3bbf94c86c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303095} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b2522e-10c4-500e-a4f6-6a85ec718f28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304604}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de7735d2-3904-5b74-a25a-790295b495b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332398" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3\\alpha x{e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7b7917-2ac5-5b52-a8e9-071c6e798cfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228087}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "036131be-8602-586a-8ee5-fe7271608cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-332723" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=6{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e688a2-c92a-5042-bfbd-f0b603a09fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e688a2-c92a-5042-bfbd-f0b603a09fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302577} :answer-field/id #uuid "3232a174-1bc9-5e8d-b848-0b6f3565c118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bf6129e-2981-5234-ae00-0e47f458eaf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332686" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=8{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-4x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x})+4\\alpha x{e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc428e2a-5a59-5fab-8d9f-a2c91fc34543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc428e2a-5a59-5fab-8d9f-a2c91fc34543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303714} :answer-field/id #uuid "152ee051-265b-51fd-9056-f2e48d9b7717" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304123}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "332fe216-05e6-5450-9057-0e365b259ef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332728" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=8{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5\\alpha x{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd9cf94f-6d2c-5279-b476-55c7434eefef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303725} {:answer/id #uuid "06570ee4-ef32-50c5-80dd-d62ee7471554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06570ee4-ef32-50c5-80dd-d62ee7471554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425856} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37a43fd-f6c9-58a0-8e79-34180df6e0a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f0a9b7d-a8ca-55a3-9a38-83e9ea72a8f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332730" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e619bfcb-6971-5f4b-bd58-575535bb5ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e619bfcb-6971-5f4b-bd58-575535bb5ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303885} :answer-field/id #uuid "22eb4a61-85b8-5926-9377-3725744df7ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304158}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1a6bf67-5f7d-58f8-b342-135abffd2994" :question/math-academy-id "q-332731" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=2{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{6x},$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x{e}^{6x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f5fd1e2-cc12-5558-a7ed-5ba45a7a9409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f5fd1e2-cc12-5558-a7ed-5ba45a7a9409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303351} :answer-field/id #uuid "4016f4c8-a669-5084-abc5-0b4e5f44e38c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304252}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f4bcc57-6f0b-5c9c-9738-574f7a44b9ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-332795" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+(2\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x}+5\\beta {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =10,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c56bae-839a-5c23-84fc-41e84582c875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48c56bae-839a-5c23-84fc-41e84582c875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302914} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a2bfcbd-53ca-5ed0-9fa5-b2f15e9a8aa7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304490}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58b63a24-88b9-5660-8634-52a128aec0c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332848" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+4(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+(\\beta +4\\beta ){e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\beta {e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-4x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =8,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305132 :question/answer-fields 