 (1-6x){e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x}+(\\beta +6\\beta ){e}^{x} & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x}+7\\beta {e}^{x} & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-6x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =30\\,\\text{and}\\,7\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =30\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=30x{e}^{-6x}+\\frac{4}{7}{e}^{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a611ead-4613-5ce9-abb7-2f7a1c9fc141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a611ead-4613-5ce9-abb7-2f7a1c9fc141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306319} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6d670d-d74d-5718-b1f2-03be1cee848e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308334}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bbd2cf3-3290-54c6-9f81-fbae34df20ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-333147" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+4(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+(\\beta +4\\beta ){e}^{x} & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\beta {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-4x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =8\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =-5,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =8\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d24b86c-53c5-54d0-b886-505e14a28f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d24b86c-53c5-54d0-b886-505e14a28f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363741} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c09edc1-d4e2-5047-b686-22ff7a25e412" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e64c2e40-5741-5178-904b-8ae1b402b5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332726" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=9{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-4\\alpha x{e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (1+4x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{4x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9xe^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9xe^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31cbcf7-0ca7-5dff-a5ff-a27c6b8e3c33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420165}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420" :question/math-academy-id "q-332708" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=9{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x})+6\\alpha x{e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{-6x}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228897 :question/id #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" :question/math-academy-id "e-21616" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x},$ and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {6x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x  e ^ {6 x} .\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08eb864c-9ccf-5d1c-909b-0c8d0c9598fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08eb864c-9ccf-5d1c-909b-0c8d0c9598fc" :answer/type {:db/id