s given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "2a0d502e-48ef-5a89-a581-fcf1fd3744f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228059}] :question/id #uuid "fcc20fdf-0331-56d0-9de8-00a5dcc17b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-332532" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{′} - 4y = 3e^{4x}$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{4x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2380dec4-7275-5f6a-8b98-9506eb15d832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2380dec4-7275-5f6a-8b98-9506eb15d832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302681} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6e6bd2-253b-5a0c-abb7-063c121b89f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304330}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "732809e5-af22-52ea-a3ee-4e7666e27b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-332801" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=20{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $20{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b80934-34f0-5c6e-9245-6b3e7d29e95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b80934-34f0-5c6e-9245-6b3e7d29e95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304008} :answer-field/id #uuid "6015dde8-efa1-50fd-8036-aa2217f861bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304339}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37ac7932-4493-598d-be89-ff6da08a57c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332895" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7543fa00-9b05-5fa7-990f-d129855f76bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :a