a ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a8abd6-1b39-5703-bc81-72f3224b9093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-2x}+6x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43a8abd6-1b39-5703-bc81-72f3224b9093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-2x}+6x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302884} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71dd12c-60df-5f92-bc42-c1396d7dc3c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304702}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c3810f2-6b8b-5c0c-b077-4ead6330565d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332899" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=6{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+6x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87aacf8e-bc15-5baf-95e2-dc84ee4735c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306441} {:answer/id #uuid "a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5x+A)e^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5x+A)e^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420162} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef304257-c02b-5b9a-91de-da2bbd172314" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308275}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cafd04c6-8f34-538e-bfbf-4314bcafa2be" :question/math-academy-id "q-333168" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-7y=5{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{7x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{7x},$ and ${e}^{7x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{7x}+7x{e}^{7x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x})-7\\alpha x{e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ (1+7x-7x)\\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4863e38f-9359-50ac-8420-a0903db6b4e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}-{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4863e38f-9359-50ac-8420-a0903db6b4e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}-{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305857} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8c50b69-1675-54dd-9a97-f08115c575e5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308198}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85a59aa1-fae1-52f3-8e86-a52159252cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333176" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=2{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha {e}^{3x}-5\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =2{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-{e}^{3x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c20186-0c39-5f43-bcc1-506ff7ca79f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c20186-0c39-5f43-bcc1-506ff7ca79f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306577} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ceb4dff-6ac3-5d7c-8f7a-742368f3039f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307710}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf174a8a-6cfc-5d83-8579-df80272afa99" :question/math-academy-id "q-333178" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=6{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{x}-4\\alpha {e}^{x} & =6{e}^{x} \\\\ -3\\alpha {e}^{x} & =6{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ef9aad9-df9f-5102-93f0-307daf1fbe9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}+4{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ef9aad9-df9f-5102-93f0-307daf1fbe9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}+4{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306209} :answer-field/id #uuid "691feb64-df85-5b59-ba1d-1f622e0cfbfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba40610c-21e4-5015-9dd9-c8ffd59bf1c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-333179" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{5x}\\cdot 5=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-3\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228898 :question/id #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21617" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-2)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =1 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9b0e257f-3041-5c27-9229-515be1b893d3